title: A mapping-position comment is dropped silently where a sequence-position one
  is refused loudly
updated: 2026-08-21T11:53:03Z
---

Found while measuring karr #148; filed rather than acted on.

ADR 0041 installed a loud guard: a document whose comment is a SEQUENCE element
cannot be written as plaintext, so decrypt_file and edit REFUSE it, naming the
path, rather than dropping the comment on the way through. That is a deliberate
trade and the POD states it.

A document whose only comment is above a MAPPING key gets neither. YAML::XS
discards the comment line before parse() sees a tree, so nothing is in the tree
for the guard to refuse, and the file is written back without it -- silently, at
exit 0.

MEASURED, sops 3.13.3, one age recipient, two plaintexts encrypted by `sops -e`:

  A: a file-leading comment, a comment above a mapping key, AND a comment above
     a sequence element
       sops -d              exit 0, all three comments back
       decrypt_file here    REFUSED: "list:0: cannot write a sops comment into
                            this document..."

  B: a file-leading comment and a comment above a mapping key, no sequence
       sops -d              exit 0, both comments back
       decrypt_file here    exit 0, plaintext written WITHOUT either comment

So the same loss is loud in one position and silent in the other, and which one
a caller gets depends on whether the document happens to contain a sequence.

NOT obviously a bug in either direction, which is why this is a ticket and not a
fix:
  * Making B loud too would refuse a document sops reads, and would need the
    read half of karr #148 (YAML::PP's raw token stream) purely to power a
    refusal.
  * Making A quiet would undo ADR 0041's decision.
  * Doing nothing is defensible and is today's state -- but it should be a
    decision, not an accident of where YAML::XS drops things.

Depends on karr #148 for the read half if the answer turns out to be "make B
loud". Lane: format, with wire as second reader (ADR 0041's guard is theirs).
